---
import SourcePanel from '../components/SourcePanel.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';
import { SOURCES } from '../data/sources';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Characters - Confirmed Cast Overview, Yuto & Hari';
const description =
  'Grave Seasons character overview using only official Steam and Blumhouse facts: Yuto and Hari, 30+ residents, 12 romance options, and the killer-candidate pool.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Characters', url: '/characters/' },
];
const sources = [SOURCES.steam, SOURCES.blumhouse, SOURCES.blumhouseMedia, SOURCES.steamDevlog];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.hari.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Character overview</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">
      Grave Seasons Characters
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      This is the short confirmed-name overview for Grave Seasons characters. Ashenridge has 30+ residents, but only a few names have appeared in official media so far, including Yuto and Hari. For logging residents, clues, and schedules once the game is out on March 9, 2027, use the <a href="/killer-decisions-and-replay/" class="text-wheat underline underline-offset-4">investigation notes guide</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      <figure>
        <img src={ASSETS.yuto.src} alt={ASSETS.yuto.alt} width={ASSETS.yuto.width} height={ASSETS.yuto.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">Official media caption: talking to Yuto in the grocery store.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={ASSETS.hari.src} alt={ASSETS.hari.alt} width={ASSETS.hari.width} height={ASSETS.hari.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">Official media caption: meeting Hari.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <p class="section-kicker">Confirmed names</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-black text-bone">Officially referenced characters</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-white/10">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[640px] border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-moss/25 text-left text-bone">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Name</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Official context</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Romance status</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-fog">
            <tr class="border-t border-white/10">
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-bone">Yuto</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Blumhouse gallery alt text references talking to Yuto in the grocery store.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Not confirmed as romanceable.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-white/10">
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-bone">Hari</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Blumhouse gallery alt text references meeting Hari.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Not confirmed as romanceable.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="prose-page mt-10">
      <h2>Why only two names are listed</h2>
      <p>
        Yuto and Hari are named in captions on the official Blumhouse media gallery: one screenshot shows the player talking to Yuto in the grocery store, another shows the player meeting Hari. Neither caption says whether they are romanceable, a potential victim, or a killer candidate, so the table above leaves those fields unconfirmed. Other names that circulate before launch without an official caption or store text behind them are not listed here.
      </p>
      <h2>What the official pages confirm about the cast</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Ashenridge has 30+ residents.</li>
        <li>There are 12 potential romanceable people.</li>
        <li>A few romanceable people may have the potential to be the killer.</li>
        <li>Characters can have storylines tied to survival and death.</li>
        <li>Every killer has a campaign, reasoning, kill styles, and targets.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That combination is what makes the cast matter: because each run picks the killer from a candidate pool, and each killer has its own targets, a resident you befriend or court may play a different role from one playthrough to the next. The developer's day-and-night devlog adds that residents keep their own schedules after dark, so where and when you meet someone is part of the investigation.
      </p>
      <h2>Tracking residents when the game launches</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Write each name exactly as the game displays it, with the place and time you first met them.</li>
        <li>Keep observed events separate from suspicions; a resident seen once near a crime scene is an observation, not a verdict.</li>
        <li>Label each record with the playthrough, since the killer and targets can change between runs.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For route information, use the <a href="/romance-guide/">romance guide</a>. For a full note-taking template, use the <a href="/killer-decisions-and-replay/">investigation notes guide</a>. For location and first-year sequencing, use the <a href="/grave-seasons-prelaunch-one-year-planner/">one-year planner</a> and the <a href="/map-of-ashenridge/">map of Ashenridge</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SourcePanel sources={sources} />
  </article>
</Layout>
